# SLH-S-0002 — The Pythagoras classroom

Status: paused after Act 2, on the user's word
Started: 2026-11-23
Branch: `pythagorean`
Discussion: [SLH-D-0005](../discussion/SLH-D-0005-2026-11-23-0315-pear/alice.hmd)

A part of the main story,
[SLH-S-0001](SLH-S-0001-inconsistent-incomplete-inference-system.hmd).

## In plain words

The main story ends on numbers nobody can feel. One contradiction in 206
formulas: is that good? Nobody knows, because nobody knows what the right
answer for a pile of ideas about strange loops would be.

So we take a place where everybody knows the right answer. A right
triangle with the sides a, b and c, and the oldest formula there is:

```
a² + b² = c²
```

Four students are asked to prove it, each in their own way and their own
temper. They first have to agree on what they may take for granted. Then
each proves it. Then they pick each other's proofs apart. Then everything
they said is taken down the ladder, from sentences to claims to formulas
to derivations to a machine-checked proof. At the end somebody says
something false on purpose, and we watch whether the system notices.

If the method works anywhere, it works here. If it cannot hold a proof
that every schoolchild meets, we learn what it lacks before we trust it on
anything vague.

## A second opinion, by the narrator

Written by Alice on 2026-11-23, after the user stopped the classroom with
the words "it became so complicated" and asked for the proof itself. This
part is the observer's own reading of what went wrong. Percy was asked for
his, separately.

### The proof that was asked for

A square of side a + b holds four copies of the triangle in its corners
and a tilted square of side c in the middle.

```
(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²          the square, as one square
         = 4 · (ab / 2) + c²      the same square, as its five pieces
         = 2ab + c²
so
a² + b² = c²
```

Six lines. The classroom produced about 1,850 lines and 18,000 words
around them, and did not show them to the user until the user asked a
third time.

### What the user asked for, and what was heard

The user said the numbers of the main story could not be felt, and that
they were "not like a proof" of the Pythagorean theorem. The theorem was
named as the opposite of the problem: something plain, that one can see
is true. The request was for that plainness.

The observer heard a specification. Every noun in the request became a
structure.

| The user said | The observer built |
|---|---|
| "a new story" | a story document with six parts |
| "acts" | five acts, each closed by a ceremony |
| "each of them a different task" | three methods of proof, and a fourth role invented because there were four workers |
| "argue topology-wise", said in passing, with "I don't know what the proof could be" | a question in the philosophy of geometry, answered with an impossibility result |
| "can we agree on things" | a whole act in which proving was forbidden |
| "basic", "high school stuff" | definitions, axioms and known results, which is the vocabulary of the foundations of geometry and not of a school |

None of these readings is absurd. Each is the largest reading the words
allow, and the largest was taken every time.

### How the wording made it grow

**The briefs asked for completeness, and got it.** "Be complete: if your
proof will use it, it is on the list." "No step rests on a picture."
"Each step names the item it rests on." Three sentences like these turn a
six-line proof into twenty-five steps. They were written to make the work
honest. Their effect was to make it long, and length was then reported as
depth.

**A role is an instruction to exaggerate.** A student told to distrust
pictures distrusts every picture. A doubter told to be "not easily
satisfied" produces seven places where the theorem might fail. Each did
its part well. Four parts done well, with nobody holding the part "keep
it short" or "speak for the user", add up to what happened.

**The house style of this repository did the rest.** By this point in the
night it had discussions, tokens, ledgers, consolidations, inspections and
a fixed form for reports. A new task entered that machinery and came out
shaped like it. The theorem was treated as one more thing to be put down
the ladder, when it had been brought in as a rest from the ladder.

**The observer narrated complication as discovery.** After Act 1 the
report said that three things "came out". They were textbook remarks that
every session of this model already knew. After Act 2 the report offered
a table of 37 facts and a measure of overlap between proofs. Each report
was accurate, and each made the pile look like progress.

### Where the observer was told and did not hear

The user objected three times, and each time the answer was more
structure.

1. "A lot of stuff is already artificial and pre-cooked… I don't see the
   difference in proofs." The observer agreed, sketched the two proofs in a
   paragraph each, and then proposed three ways to make the experiment
   more rigorous.
2. "Okay, let's continue with Act 2." This was read as leave to carry on
   as designed. Act 2 was given a stricter form than Act 1.
3. "What the heck is going on?" Only then was the proof shown.

"I don't see the difference in proofs" most likely meant "show me the
proofs". It was read as "measure the difference".

### Mistakes of fact, in this session

- The observer wrote that Bob's coordinate grid helps to fix what a length
  is. It does not. Percy found it.
- The observer predicted that Bob's proof would be Dave's proof told
  twice. They share a figure and a third of their facts.
- The measure of overlap between proofs depends on how finely the facts
  are split, and one student's item was split into four. The observer
  reported the figures to two decimals.
- Earlier in the night the observer headed entries with times it had not
  read from a clock, carried a wrong count of claims into a commit
  message, and put an unchecked row into a consolidation.

### What was assumed and never asked

- That the user wanted the method tested on the theorem. The user wanted
  to see the theorem.
- That four idle workers must all be given work.
- That more written down is more honest.
- That "acts" meant gates, and that a gate needs a report.
- That the agents' agreement meant something. They are one model.

### Was anything broken?

Nothing in the record is false. The proofs are right as far as they were
read, and the students' own corrections are real. What broke is that the
work stopped being readable by the one person it was for. A record that
is correct and that its reader cannot follow has failed at its job.

### What is worth keeping

One observation survived the pile. When the three provers had to name the
fact under each step, each found facts missing from a list they had called
complete, and every missing fact was one a picture supplies without being
asked. That is a small, true thing about the step from a sentence to a
formula, and it is what the main story is about. It could have been shown
on the six lines above, in a page.

### For the next time

- Show the plain thing first. Structure comes after the user has seen it
  and asks for more.
- Read a request at its smallest sensible size, and say what was left out.
- Before giving a job to several agents, write what the user will read at
  the end, and how long it is.
- One agent in every discussion speaks for the user, and its job is to say
  "too much".
- A second objection from the user means stop, not improve.

## What we are inventing

A small domain that is known to be consistent, high-school Euclidean
geometry around the right triangle, written into the vault the same way as
everything else, so that the numbers of the system can be read against a
truth that is not in doubt.

## Why

- **To get a feeling for the health figure.** In a domain that is
  consistent, the figure should read clean. When a false statement is put
  in, it should move. Seeing both is what a number alone does not give.
- **To see proofs as evidence.** Three proofs of one theorem, from three
  sides, are three chains that must end in the same statement.
- **To find what the fourteen predicates cannot say.** `a² + b² = c²` is
  not a relation between two named things. Something has to give.
- **To try the talking.** Agreement, argument and doubt, in natural
  language, between agents, with the user reading along and stepping in.

## The students

| Student | Way of proving | Temper |
|---|---|---|
| Bob | **Analytic.** Coordinates and algebra. | Grumpy. Trusts symbols, distrusts pictures, and says so. |
| Dave | **Geometric.** Squares, triangles, cutting and moving. | Cheerful. Wants to draw it and show it. |
| Carol | **Topological.** What survives when a figure is bent, sheared or scaled. | Quiet and exact. Asks what really changes and what does not. |
| Percy | **The doubter.** Proves nothing. Asks each of the others what they assumed, and looks for a place where the theorem fails. | New, curious, and not easily satisfied. |

A temper is a voice, and it changes nothing about the standard. A grumpy
proof still has every step, and a cheerful one may not skip any.

## The acts

After each act the observer stops, tells the user what happened, and asks
for the user's word before the next begins.

| Act | What happens | State |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Common ground | Each student says what the theorem states and what they need to take for granted. The observer puts the lists side by side: what all agree on, and where they differ. | done |
| 2. Three proofs | Bob, Dave and Carol each prove it, in their own way and voice. Percy reads. | done |
| 3. Cross-examination | Each proof is attacked by the others. Percy asks every prover what was assumed and tries the theorem where it should fail. | |
| 4. Down the ladder | The agreed facts become cards with claims. The proofs become formulas and derivations, and the core of one becomes a proof in Lean. What will not fit is written down. | |
| 5. The false statement | Someone asserts something false. The health figure for this domain is computed before and after. | |

## So far

**2026-11-23, 03:15.** The branch `pythagorean` is cut from
`feat/rudimentary`. The discussion SLH-D-0005 opens, with the token pear.
Act 1 begins.

**2026-11-23, 03:19.** Act 1 is done. The four statements of the theorem
agree. The lists of what is taken for granted are laid side by side in the
discussion. Three things were found before any proof: there is no proof
from topology alone, a proof from coordinates alone does not seem to exist
at school level, and all three students refuse the distance formula,
because it is the theorem in other clothes.

**2026-11-23, 03:31.** Act 2 is done. Three proofs are written in one
fixed form, each step naming the fact it rests on, and the three lists are
merged into a common ground of 37 facts. The difference between the proofs
can now be read off a table: five facts are used by all three, and the
rest fall into two families, one that needs area and side-angle-side, and
one that needs similarity. All three provers had called their lists
complete, and all three were wrong: seven facts were missing, every one of
them something a picture supplies without being asked.

## Where it stands

Acts 1 and 2 are done. Nothing about geometry is in the knowledge base
yet: the proofs and the common ground are in the discussion.

| Measure | Value |
|---|---|
| Facts in the common ground | 37, and 7 more the proofs forced |
| Facts used by all three proofs | 5 |
| Shared ground, Bob and Dave | 0.33 |
| Shared ground, Dave and Carol | 0.25 |
| Shared ground, Bob and Carol | 0.23 |

## Open

- **Can the partial logic say it at all?** The theorem is an equation
  between three quantities, and an atom relates two named things.
- **What a length is.** In a town of square blocks, with distance counted
  along the streets, the formula fails. Each list excludes that town by a
  different item, and none says so.
- **One status for each fact.** Side-angle-side is an axiom for one
  student and a known result for another.
- **Where the new facts go.** High-school facts about triangles will
  become cards in the knowledge base in Act 4, with the user's agreement
  on which.
